\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{The form domain is dense for the measure}
\date{2026-11-07}

\p{With #{F} and #{\mu} as in \ref{fdip-00F1}, the domain
#{F} is dense in #{L^2(X,\mu)}. The subspace
#{F_0=\{u\in F:u=0\text{ on }V_0\}} is dense as well.}

\proof{
\p{Every open set in #{\Sigma} is a countable union of
cylinders. Successive differences in the algebra of
\ref{fdip-00EV} make the union disjoint. A finite partial
union is a cylinder function and converges in #{L^2} to the
open indicator. A nested difference of indicators is their
difference in #{L^2}, and an increasing union is their
#{L^2} limit, so the Borel sets whose indicators lie in
that closure form a difference system in the sense of
\ref{fdip-00F8}. They contain the open sets. That
theorem identifies the smallest such system with the Borel
#{\sigma}-algebra, so every Borel indicator lies in the
closure. Cylinder functions are dense in #{L^2(\Sigma,\nu)}.}

\p{A function on #{X} pulls back to a function constant on
the fibers of #{\pi}. By \ref{fdip-00EY} a cylinder indicator
agrees almost everywhere with the pullback of the
corresponding cell indicator. Cell indicators are therefore
dense in #{L^2(X,\mu)}.}

\p{Let #{C} be the image of a cell and let #{J} be its finite
intersection with the other cells of that length. Let #{U}
be the union of the level-#{m} cells that meet #{J}, with
#{m} larger than the length of #{C}. By \ref{fdip-00EY} the
measure of #{U} tends to zero with #{m}. The cells of
level #{m} that miss #{J} are compact and disjoint from
#{J}, so the distance from #{J} to their union is positive
and #{U} contains a neighborhood of #{J}. Cells of one
length meet only inside #{J}. Every point of
#{C\setminus U} therefore has a neighborhood that stays in
#{C\setminus U}. The closure of #{C\setminus U} and the
closure of #{X\setminus(C\cup U)} are disjoint and compact:
a common point would lie in #{J}, which those sets avoid.
Resistance distance to the two closures defines a continuous
function equal to #{1} on the first and equal to #{0} on
the second. The function and the indicator of #{C} differ
only on #{U}, so their square distance in #{L^2} is at
most #{\mu(U)}. \ref{fdip-00EP} supplies a function of #{F}
uniformly close to that continuous function, hence close in
#{L^2}.}

\p{For the subspace vanishing on #{V_0}, fix #{u\in F} and
#{n\ge 1}. Let #{\psi} be the energy-minimizing extension,
given by the trace in \ref{fdip-00EI}, of the values #{u}
on #{V_0} and #{0} on the rest of #{V_n}. At every later
vertex the graph equation is a weighted average of the
neighbors, so the maximum and the minimum lie on #{V_n}.
Thus #{|\psi|} is at most the maximum of #{|u|} on #{V_0}.
Only the three level-#{n} cells that contain the corners
carry a nonzero value, and \ref{fdip-00EY} makes their
measures tend to zero. Hence #{\psi} tends to zero in
#{L^2}, while #{u-\psi} vanishes on #{V_0} and lies in
#{F}.}
}
